NOTES AND QUESTIONS ON AMOUNT IN CONTROVERSY IN REMOVAL CASES AND ST. PAUL MERCURY V. RED CAB
1. In 2011, section 1446(c)(2) was added to the removal statute. Reread section 1446(c)(2). What complications related to the removal of cases based on diversity jurisdiction does it solve? Does it address situations like those in St. Paul Mercury, or does it address a different set of problems altogether?

2. What incentives does the amount-in-controversy requirement create for parties in removed cases? Do you see how making arguments about the amount in controversy in a removed case requires considerable delicacy for both parties’ attorneys? Consider the remark from the court in Shaw v. Dow Brands, Inc., 994 F.2d 364, 366 (7th Cir. 1993), that “at oral argument we had the privilege of witnessing a comic scene: plaintiff’s personal injury lawyer protests up and down that his client’s injuries are as minor and insignificant as can be, while attorneys for the manufacturer paint a sob story about how plaintiff’s life has been wrecked.”

3. The Court in St. Paul Mercury emphasizes that “events occurring subsequent to removal which reduce the amount recoverable … do not oust the district court’s jurisdiction once attached.” This is an instance of a broader principle, the time-of-filing rule, which we encountered in ConnectU v. Zuckerberg. The rule is that “the jurisdiction of the Court depends upon the state of things at the time of the action brought,” or in the case of removed actions, at the time of removal. There are some exceptions to this rule, such as when a non-diverse party is added (or dismissed) from an action, which may destroy (or salvage) diversity jurisdiction. This principle becomes important to our next two cases, Caterpillar and Grupo Dataflux.
